    27	#      are asymmetric: an earlier frame finishes first, so its trail is frozen; when a
    28	#      later frame hits that frozen trail, the later continuation inherits the older trail
    29	#      color while the older trail itself remains unchanged. This is the concrete meaning
    30	#      of "the first-fired color transfers to the later-fired ray".
    31	#    - Small trace:
    32	#      * Base task: a central red frame and a farther blue frame would fire together; if
    33	#        their rays meet, both continuations react symmetrically and each keeps its own
    34	#        color.
    35	#      * D14 alone: the red frame fires first, freezes, and the blue ray just stops when
    36	#        it reaches red's trail; no cross-frame color cue appears.
    37	#      * Fused combo: the red frame still fires first, but when the blue ray later reaches
    38	#        the frozen red trail, the blue continuation inherits red and bends according to
    39	#        the same local collision geometry. The frozen red trail stays in place, so order
    40	#        decides the direction of color transfer.
    41	# b) Complexity calibration
    42	#    - The fusion is intentionally limited to one ordering signal and one transfer rule:
    43	#      center distance controls who can donate color, and donor color is always the color
    44	#      currently visible on the earlier trail cell/segment that was hit.
    45	#    - The generator uses mostly 2-3 single-gap frames. That keeps examples sparse enough
    46	#      that a human can see which frame is more central, which later ray touched it, and
    47	#      where the trail color changes after that touch.
    48	#    - We reject samples that only exhibit within-frame color swaps; at least one visible
    49	#      cross-frame transfer must occur, otherwise the combo collapses back toward either
    50	#      plain D3 or plain D14.
    51	# c) Implicit assumption audit
    52	#    - Base/D14 assumption broken by D3: `beam.color` cannot be constant. The solver must
    53	#      track mutable current color plus immutable `source_color` for diagnostics.
    54	#    - D14 assumption broken by the fusion: earlier trails cannot be mere solid blockers.
    55	#      They must retain direction and visible color metadata so a later ray can collide
    56	#      with them asymmetrically.
    57	#    - Base 90-degree rule erased the meeting cell. That is impossible when the donor cell
    58	#      belongs to a frozen earlier frame. The fused solver therefore keeps the older cell
    59	#      intact and only redirects the later continuation; this is the key new asymmetry.
    60	#    - Base ownership tables assumed every visited path cell is writable by the current
    61	#      beam. That fails when a later beam's 45-degree collision point is an already-frozen
    62	#      cell. The implementation therefore allows a beam path to include "traversed but not
    63	#      owned" positions so future direction can continue from a frozen collision point.
    64	# d) Placement legality
    65	#    - Frames still obey the original 4x4 geometry, non-overlap spacing, and in-bounds
    66	#      constraints.
    67	#    - Candidate gaps are validated against intact full-frame obstacles so every emitted ray
    68	#      has runway through background cells before any beam painting starts.
    69	#    - Generation also enforces distinct frame-center distances and checks the solver on
    70	#      every candidate, guaranteeing that all new collision assumptions used by `main()`
    71	#      are physically realizable on produced inputs.
    72	# e) Visual observability
    73	#    - Order depends only on visible geometry relative to the grid center; no numeric color
    74	#      ids or hidden randomness influence it.
    75	#    - Color transfer is also structural rather than numeric: later segments take the color
    76	#      of the earlier trail they visibly touch. Because block colors are distinct, the cue
    77	#      can be inferred from just a few examples.
    78	#    - The generator rejects candidates where the fused output matches either the D14
    79	#      stop-on-static baseline or the original simultaneous baseline too closely; this keeps
    80	#      the fusion effect visible rather than theoretical.
    81	# f) Visual neatness
    82	#    - Single-gap generation avoids dense self-intersections and makes the centrality cue
    83	#      legible. The output is usually one earlier trail plus one or more later trails that
    84	#      visibly change color after touching it.
    85	#    - Frame spacing and runway checks prevent frames from crowding the same local area, so
    86	#      the eye can separate the donor trail from the inherited continuation.
    87	# g) New solver assumptions and generator guarantees
    88	#    - Assumption: if a later ray hits a frozen trail at 45 or 90 degrees, the frozen trail
    89	#      carries enough metadata (enter direction, segment direction, visible color) to define
    90	#      the donor. Guaranteed because earlier batches record those maps explicitly.
    91	#    - Assumption: generator outputs contain at least one visible cross-frame transfer and a
    92	#      nontrivial difference from both simpler baselines. Guaranteed by `variant_metrics()`
    93	#      filters in `_find_interacting_grid()`.
    94	#    - Assumption: later rays never need to overwrite frozen cells. Guaranteed by the fused
    95	#      collision semantics, which either continue from the frozen collision point without
    96	#      repainting it or redirect before it.
