   870	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   871	    """
   872	    Fire framed-block rays in center-distance order; later rays inherit color from earlier trails they hit.
   873	
   874	    Input structure:
   875	    - The grid is square with one dominant background color. Each object is a monochrome 2x2 block enclosed by a shared-color 4x4 frame.
   876	    - Missing frame-border cells are structural gaps. Every gap launches one outward ray determined by its side or corner position.
   877	
   878	    Rule:
   879	    - Sort frames by distance from the grid center, nearest first; if a tie ever appears, break it top-to-bottom then left-to-right.
   880	    - Process one frame at a time. Inside that frame, keep the original local ray geometry: 90-degree meetings redirect along the bisector, and 45-degree meetings merge into the same zigzag motif.
   881	    - Finished trails from earlier frames stay frozen. When a later ray hits one of those frozen trail cells or diagonal segments at a valid collision angle, the later continuation adopts the older trail's color and bends accordingly, while the older trail remains unchanged.
   882	    - Cells painted before a collision keep their old color. Only the continuation after the collision uses the inherited color.
   883	
   884	    Key insight:
   885	    - Solving now requires tracking one chronology that couples order and color: central frames act first, and their completed trails are not just blockers but color donors that redirect farther rays.
   886	    """
   887	    return _solve(grid, sequential=True, cross_frame_inherit=True)
